    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Moving to Houston means trading a state income tax for higher property taxes, getting used to long, humid summers, and learning to live with hurricanes, flood risk and a car-first city. In return you get a huge job market, a wide range of housing and some of the most diverse food and culture in the country.</p>
<p>This guide covers what newcomers most often wish they had known: taxes, Houston's famous lack of zoning, weather, traffic, neighborhoods, cost of living and how to set up electricity. It ends with a practical relocation checklist.</p>

<h2>Taxes: No Income Tax, But Watch Property Taxes</h2>
<p>Texas does not tax individual personal income. In 2019, Texas voters approved a constitutional amendment that explicitly bans a state personal income tax. For many people moving from high-tax states, that is the headline.</p>
<p>The trade-off is property tax. Texas has no state property tax, but counties, cities, school districts, community colleges, hospital districts and special districts such as municipal utility districts (MUDs) each levy their own. Total rates vary widely from one neighborhood to the next, so two similar homes can have very different tax bills.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Homestead exemption:</strong> Once you own and live in your home, apply for the residence homestead exemption with your county appraisal district (HCAD in Harris County). As of the 2025 tax year, the school district homestead exemption is $140,000. See our <a href="/blog/texas-homestead-exemption/">Texas homestead exemption guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Protests:</strong> You can protest your appraised value each year. The usual deadline is May 15 or 30 days after your appraisal notice, whichever is later, according to the <a href="https://comptroller.texas.gov/taxes/property-tax/protests/">Texas Comptroller</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Budget for it:</strong> Lenders often escrow property taxes and insurance into your monthly payment, so estimate both before you shop.</li>
</ul>
<p>[[tool:texas-property-tax-calculator]]</p>
<p>A CPA can explain how a Texas move changes your overall tax situation.</p>

<h2>No Zoning: What It Means for Homebuyers</h2>
<p>Houston is the largest U.S. city without conventional zoning. Voters rejected zoning proposals in 1948, 1962 and 1993. That does not mean anything goes: the city regulates lot sizes and building lines, and it can enforce private deed restrictions in some neighborhoods.</p>
<p>For buyers, deed restrictions do much of the work zoning does elsewhere. Some neighborhoods have strong, actively enforced restrictions and an active civic club or HOA. Others have weak or expired restrictions, which means a townhome cluster, a business or a commercial building could appear next door.</p>
<ul>
<li>Read the deed restrictions and HOA documents before you buy, not after.</li>
<li>Drive the surrounding blocks and look at vacant lots and older properties likely to be redeveloped.</li>
<li>Ask your title company and agent whether restrictions are current and enforced.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Heat, Humidity and What They Do to a House</h2>
<p>Houston summers are long, hot and humid, and the heat often lingers well into fall. Air conditioning is not optional, and your electric bill will reflect it in summer.</p>
<p>Humidity also affects the house itself. Mold, mildew and moisture problems are common when HVAC systems are undersized or poorly maintained. Change air filters regularly, keep gutters and drainage working, and consider a dehumidifier for closets or problem rooms.</p>
<p>[[product:midea-dehumidifier]]</p>
<p>Our <a href="/blog/houston-home-maintenance-checklist/">Houston home maintenance checklist</a> lists the seasonal tasks that matter most in this climate.</p>

<h2>Hurricanes, Flooding and Power Outages</h2>
<p>The Atlantic hurricane season runs from June 1 to November 30. Houston is far enough inland to avoid the worst storm surge in most neighborhoods, but heavy rain and wind still cause major damage. Hurricane Harvey in 2017 flooded thousands of homes across the region, many outside mapped high-risk flood zones.</p>
<p>Power outages are the other big risk. When Hurricane Beryl hit in July 2024, CenterPoint Energy's outage count peaked at about 2.2 million customers, and many homes went without power for days in summer heat. The February 2021 winter storm also caused widespread outages across Texas.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Flood insurance:</strong> Standard homeowners policies exclude flood. Look up any address on FEMA's flood maps and get a quote. NFIP policies generally have a 30-day waiting period, so do not wait for a storm.</li>
<li><strong>Windstorm coverage:</strong> Check your homeowners policy's wind and hail deductible, which is often a percentage of the dwelling coverage.</li>
<li><strong>Outage plan:</strong> Have a battery or generator backup plan, a NOAA weather radio, water and a way to keep phones charged.</li>
</ul>
<p>[[product:portable-power-station]]</p>
<p>If you are buying, read our guide to <a href="/blog/buying-a-house-in-a-flood-zone-houston/">buying a house in a flood zone in Houston</a> before you make an offer.</p>

<h2>Traffic and Car Dependence</h2>
<p>Houston is spread out and built around freeways. Most residents drive to work, school and errands. METRO runs buses and light rail, and some inner-Loop neighborhoods are walkable, but outside the core, plan on a car for nearly every trip.</p>
<p>Commute time should drive your neighborhood choice. Major job centers include downtown, the Texas Medical Center, the Energy Corridor, the Galleria/Uptown area, the Port of Houston and NASA's Johnson Space Center in Clear Lake. A home that looks cheap can cost you hours each week if it is on the wrong side of town. Test the commute at rush hour before you sign anything.</p>
<p>New residents generally have 30 days to register a vehicle in Texas and 90 days to get a Texas driver license, according to the Texas Department of Motor Vehicles.</p>

<h2>Neighborhoods and Suburbs</h2>
<p>Houston offers very different lifestyles within one metro area. Inside the Loop, neighborhoods such as Montrose, the Heights, Midtown and Rice Village offer older homes, townhomes, restaurants and shorter commutes, usually with smaller lots and higher prices per square foot.</p>
<p>Outside the Loop and in the suburbs, you will find larger homes, newer construction and master-planned communities with pools and trails, often paired with longer drives, HOA fees and MUD taxes.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Area type</th><th>Typical housing</th><th>Commute</th><th>Watch for</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Inner Loop</td><td>Older bungalows, townhomes, condos</td><td>Shorter to central job centers</td><td>Older homes, smaller lots, weak deed restrictions in some areas</td></tr>
<tr><td>Outside the Loop, inside Beltway 8</td><td>Mid-century and newer subdivisions</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Street-level flood history, aging infrastructure</td></tr>
<tr><td>Suburbs and master-planned communities</td><td>Newer single-family homes</td><td>Longer, often freeway- or toll-dependent</td><td>MUD taxes, HOA fees and rules, drainage design</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>For families, our guide to the <a href="/blog/best-houston-suburbs-for-families/">best Houston suburbs for families</a> compares Katy, Sugar Land, The Woodlands, Pearland and more.</p>

<h2>Cost of Living in Houston</h2>
<p>Houston is often described as more affordable than many other large U.S. metros, especially for housing. Newcomers from coastal cities frequently find they can afford more space. Still, "affordable" depends on what you compare and which costs you count.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Usually lower:</strong> home prices and rents compared with many large coastal metros, and no state income tax.</li>
<li><strong>Often higher than expected:</strong> property taxes, homeowners and flood insurance, summer electricity bills and car costs.</li>
<li><strong>Varies by area:</strong> HOA fees, MUD taxes and toll road costs.</li>
</ul>
<p>Unsure whether to rent first? Many newcomers rent for six to twelve months to learn the city before buying. Our <a href="/tools/rent-vs-buy-calculator/">rent vs. buy calculator</a> helps you compare the two.</p>

<h2>Setting Up Utilities: Texas's Deregulated Electricity Market</h2>
<p>Most of the Houston area is in Texas's competitive retail electricity market. You do not buy power from CenterPoint Energy. CenterPoint owns the poles and wires, reads meters and restores outages, but you choose a retail electric provider (REP) and a plan.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Confirm your area has choice.</strong> Some areas served by municipal utilities or electric cooperatives are not part of the competitive market.</li>
<li><strong>Compare plans.</strong> The Public Utility Commission of Texas runs PowerToChoose.org, the official site for comparing plans from registered providers.</li>
<li><strong>Read the Electricity Facts Label.</strong> Check the price at different usage levels, contract length, early termination fees and any minimum-usage charges.</li>
<li><strong>Watch your contract end date.</strong> When a fixed-rate contract expires, you may roll onto a more expensive variable rate unless you choose a new plan.</li>
</ol>
<p>Water, sewer and trash service usually come from the city or your MUD. Gas service, if the home has it, comes from the local gas utility.</p>

<h2>Moving to Houston Checklist</h2>
<ol>
<li>Visit in summer, if you can, and test your commute at rush hour.</li>
<li>Shortlist neighborhoods by commute, schools, tax rate and flood history.</li>
<li>Look up addresses on FEMA's flood maps and get insurance quotes, including flood.</li>
<li>Read deed restrictions and HOA documents before you sign.</li>
<li>Set up electricity through a provider you chose, plus water, internet and gas.</li>
<li>Register your vehicle within 30 days and get a Texas driver license within 90 days.</li>
<li>Apply for your homestead exemption once you own and occupy the home.</li>
<li>Build a hurricane and outage kit before June 1.</li>
<li>Sign up for local emergency alerts and learn your evacuation zone.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Bottom Line</h2>
<p>Moving to Houston pays off for many people: no state income tax, plenty of jobs and more house for the money. Go in clear-eyed about property taxes, flood and hurricane risk, summer heat, no zoning and long commutes. Pick your neighborhood by commute and flood history first, set up your electricity plan wisely, and prepare for storm season before it arrives.</p>
